    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Denudation</span> is the general wearing down and removal of material from the Earth's surface by natural agents, gradually lowering and reshaping the land over time.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Denudation</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Denudation is the combined effect of processes that wear away and transport rock and soil from higher areas, exposing the underlying land to further change.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Agents of Denudation</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Agent</th><th>How it works</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Running water</td><td>Rivers and streams erode, transport and deposit sediment, especially in humid regions</td></tr><tr><td>Wind</td><td>Picks up and carries loose particles, especially effective in arid and semi-arid areas</td></tr><tr><td>Ice (glaciers)</td><td>Grinds and plucks rock as it moves slowly downhill, significant in cold/high-altitude regions</td></tr><tr><td>Waves</td><td>Erode and shape coastlines through the constant action of breaking water</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. The Four Processes of Denudation</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Process</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Weathering</td><td>The breakdown of rocks in situ (in place), without significant movement</td></tr><tr><td>Erosion</td><td>The wearing away and removal of rock/soil material by moving agents</td></tr><tr><td>Transportation</td><td>The movement of eroded material from one place to another</td></tr><tr><td>Deposition</td><td>The dropping or settling of transported material when the agent loses energy</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. The Denudation Cycle</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">These four processes work together in sequence: material is first broken down (weathering), then removed (erosion), carried elsewhere (transportation), and finally laid down (deposition) — continuously reshaping landforms over geological time.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Significance of Denudation</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Shapes major landforms such as valleys, plains and coastlines.</li><li>Contributes to soil formation through the breakdown of parent rock.</li><li>Can cause land degradation when erosion is excessive.</li><li>Influences agriculture, construction and settlement patterns.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the order: weathering happens first in place, then erosion, transportation and deposition move and redeposit the material.</div>",
