    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Weathering</span> is the in-situ breakdown and decay of rocks at or near the Earth's surface, caused by exposure to the atmosphere, water, temperature changes and living organisms.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Types of Weathering</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mechanical (physical)</td><td>Physical disintegration of rock without chemical change</td><td>Frost shattering, exfoliation (onion-skin weathering), thermal expansion/contraction</td></tr><tr><td>Chemical</td><td>Decomposition of rock through chemical reactions</td><td>Oxidation, carbonation, hydrolysis, solution</td></tr><tr><td>Biological</td><td>Breakdown caused by the actions of living organisms</td><td>Plant root growth splitting rock, burrowing animals, lichens releasing acids</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Factors Influencing Weathering</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li><strong>Climate:</strong> temperature range and rainfall strongly affect both mechanical and chemical weathering rates.</li><li><strong>Rock type and structure:</strong> some rocks (e.g. limestone) weather faster than others (e.g. granite); joints and cracks speed up weathering.</li><li><strong>Vegetation cover:</strong> plant roots and organic acids accelerate biological and chemical weathering.</li><li><strong>Relief and slope:</strong> steep slopes encourage faster removal of weathered material, exposing fresh rock.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Importance of Weathering</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Breaks down rock into smaller particles that eventually form soil.</li><li>Releases minerals and nutrients important for plant growth.</li><li>Shapes distinctive landforms such as inselbergs, scree slopes and karst features.</li><li>Provides raw material transported by erosion to form new landforms elsewhere.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Negative Effects of Weathering</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Can weaken building foundations and structures over time.</li><li>Contributes to rockfall and landslide hazards.</li><li>Excessive weathering combined with erosion can degrade agricultural land.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be able to distinguish mechanical, chemical and biological weathering with a specific example of each — this is a common exam requirement.</div>",
