    "topic": "Mass Movements",
    "topicCode": "SS3-GEO-03",
    "module": "Physical Geography",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Mass movement</span> (or mass wasting) is the downslope movement of weathered rock, soil and debris under the direct influence of gravity, with little or no help from a transporting medium like water or ice.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Types of Mass Movement</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Soil creep</td><td>Very slow, gradual downslope movement of soil, often imperceptible except over long periods</td></tr><tr><td>Landslides</td><td>Rapid movement of a mass of rock or soil down a slope, often triggered suddenly</td></tr><tr><td>Rockfall</td><td>Sudden falling of rock fragments from a steep slope or cliff face</td></tr><tr><td>Mudflow</td><td>Fast-moving flow of mud and water-saturated debris down a slope</td></tr><tr><td>Solifluction</td><td>Slow flowing of waterlogged soil over permanently frozen ground, common in cold climates</td></tr><tr><td>Avalanches</td><td>Rapid movement of large masses of snow (and sometimes rock/soil) down a mountainside</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Causes of Mass Movement</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Steepness of slope — steeper slopes are more prone to movement.</li><li>Heavy or prolonged rainfall saturating soil and reducing its stability.</li><li>Earthquakes and vibrations that trigger sudden slope failure.</li><li>Removal of vegetation (deforestation), which weakens soil cohesion.</li><li>Undercutting of slopes by rivers, waves or human excavation.</li><li>Freeze-thaw action weakening rock and soil structure.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Effects of Mass Movement on Human Activities</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Destruction of farmland, roads, and buildings in affected areas.</li><li>Loss of lives in severe landslide or avalanche events.</li><li>Disruption of transportation and communication networks.</li><li>Siltation of rivers and reservoirs from deposited debris.</li><li>Displacement of communities living on or near unstable slopes.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Reducing the Risk of Mass Movement</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Avoiding construction on steep, unstable slopes.</li><li>Planting vegetation to stabilise soil.</li><li>Building retaining walls and proper drainage systems.</li><li>Monitoring slopes in high-risk areas for early warning signs.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember that mass movement is driven mainly by gravity, distinguishing it from erosion, which requires a transporting agent such as water, wind or ice.</div>",
