    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Climate</span> refers to the average weather conditions of a place over a long period (usually 30 years or more), while <span class=\"learn-keyword\">climate change</span> refers to significant, lasting shifts in these patterns.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Climate and Climate Change</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Climate change is a long-term alteration in temperature, precipitation and other atmospheric conditions, distinct from short-term weather variation, and can occur at global or local scales.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Historical and Contemporary Evidence</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Type of evidence</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Historical</td><td>Ice cores, tree rings, fossil records, and sediment layers showing past climate conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Contemporary</td><td>Rising global temperature records, melting glaciers and ice caps, shifting rainfall patterns, more frequent extreme weather events</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Causes of Climate Change</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Natural causes</td><td>Volcanic eruptions, variations in solar radiation, changes in Earth's orbit, natural greenhouse gas fluctuations</td></tr><tr><td>Human (anthropogenic) causes</td><td>Burning of fossil fuels, deforestation, industrial emissions, gas flaring, intensive agriculture producing methane</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Effects of Climate Change</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li><strong>Environment:</strong> rising sea levels, melting ice caps, more frequent droughts and floods, loss of biodiversity.</li><li><strong>Agriculture:</strong> shifting growing seasons, reduced yields in some regions, desertification in parts of Nigeria's North.</li><li><strong>Human activities:</strong> displacement of coastal and vulnerable communities, increased health risks, economic losses from extreme weather.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Global and Local Responses</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li><strong>Global:</strong> international agreements such as the Paris Agreement, promotion of renewable energy, carbon emission reduction targets.</li><li><strong>Local (Nigeria):</strong> afforestation programmes (e.g. the Great Green Wall), climate-smart agriculture, reduction of gas flaring, public awareness campaigns.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be ready to distinguish natural from human causes of climate change, and give at least one Nigerian example of an effect and a response.</div>",
