    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\">The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS)</span> is a regional organisation formed to promote economic integration and cooperation among West African countries.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Formation and Objectives</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">ECOWAS was formed on 28 May 1975 through the Treaty of Lagos, led by Nigeria and Togo, with the main objective of promoting economic cooperation and integration among West African states to raise living standards and foster development.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Structure of ECOWAS</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Organ</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Authority of Heads of State and Government</td><td>The supreme decision-making body</td></tr><tr><td>Council of Ministers</td><td>Advises the Authority and oversees policy implementation</td></tr><tr><td>ECOWAS Commission</td><td>The executive and administrative arm, based in Abuja, Nigeria</td></tr><tr><td>Community Parliament</td><td>A legislative and advisory body</td></tr><tr><td>Community Court of Justice</td><td>Interprets the treaty and settles disputes among member states</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Functions of ECOWAS</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Promoting free movement of persons, goods, services and capital among member states.</li><li>Harmonising trade and economic policies across the region.</li><li>Coordinating infrastructure development (roads, telecommunications, energy).</li><li>Supporting peace, security and conflict resolution within West Africa.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Achievements of ECOWAS</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Implementation of the ECOWAS passport and free movement protocol.</li><li>Peacekeeping interventions through ECOMOG in Liberia and Sierra Leone.</li><li>Progress toward a common external tariff and regional trade facilitation.</li><li>Increased regional cooperation on infrastructure and energy projects.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Challenges of ECOWAS</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Language barriers among English, French and Portuguese-speaking member states.</li><li>Poor transport and communication infrastructure linking member states.</li><li>Political instability, coups and member withdrawals affecting unity.</li><li>Overlapping memberships in other regional organisations.</li><li>Weak implementation of agreed policies by some member states.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">6. Role in Regional Integration</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">ECOWAS plays a central role in regional integration by harmonising policy, facilitating trade and movement, and fostering a shared sense of West African identity and cooperative development.</p><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Know the formation date (1975), the Treaty of Lagos, and the location of the ECOWAS Commission (Abuja) as commonly tested facts.</div>",
