    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Trade</span> is the exchange of goods and services between individuals, regions or countries, forming a fundamental part of economic activity and development.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning and Types of Trade</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Internal (domestic) trade</td><td>Trade carried out within the boundaries of a single country</td></tr><tr><td>International trade</td><td>Trade carried out between different countries, involving imports and exports</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"learn-p\">Internal trade includes retail and wholesale trade, while international trade involves exporting (selling goods abroad) and importing (buying goods from abroad).</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Importance of Trade</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Enables countries to obtain goods and resources they lack.</li><li>Generates revenue and foreign exchange earnings.</li><li>Creates employment in production, transportation and commerce.</li><li>Promotes specialisation, improving efficiency and quality of goods.</li><li>Encourages cultural exchange and international cooperation.</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Trade Patterns in Nigeria and West Africa</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nigeria's exports</td><td>Crude oil and petroleum products dominate; also cocoa, cashew and other agricultural products</td></tr><tr><td>Nigeria's imports</td><td>Machinery, vehicles, refined petroleum products, and manufactured goods</td></tr><tr><td>Intra-West African trade</td><td>Significant informal cross-border trade in agricultural produce, livestock and manufactured goods, alongside formal ECOWAS-facilitated trade</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Problems of Trade</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Poor transport and infrastructure increasing the cost of moving goods.</li><li>Over-dependence on a narrow range of export products (e.g. oil for Nigeria).</li><li>Trade barriers such as tariffs, quotas and complex customs procedures.</li><li>Currency instability affecting the cost and predictability of trade.</li><li>Smuggling and informal trade reducing government revenue.</li><li>Inadequate market information and standardisation across borders.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be able to explain Nigeria's over-dependence on oil exports as both a trade pattern and a trade problem — this connection is frequently tested.</div>",
