    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\">A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Geographic Information System (GIS)</span> is a computer-based system designed to capture, store, manipulate, analyse and display geographically referenced (spatial) data.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of GIS</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">GIS integrates hardware, software and data to allow users to visualise, question, analyse and interpret data to understand relationships, patterns and trends in geographic terms.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Components of GIS</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hardware</td><td>Computers, servers, GPS devices and other equipment used to run GIS software and store data</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Programs that provide the functions and tools needed to store, analyse and display geographic information</td></tr><tr><td>Data</td><td>Spatial (location-based) and attribute (descriptive) data that form the core content analysed in GIS</td></tr><tr><td>People</td><td>Skilled GIS professionals who design, manage and use the system effectively</td></tr><tr><td>Methods</td><td>Well-designed plans and rules, including data models and analysis procedures, unique to each organisation using GIS</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Applications of GIS</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Application area</th><th>Example use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Resource management</td><td>Mapping and managing forests, water resources and mineral deposits</td></tr><tr><td>Urban and regional planning</td><td>Planning road networks, zoning and infrastructure development</td></tr><tr><td>Environmental studies</td><td>Monitoring pollution, land degradation and climate-related changes</td></tr><tr><td>Disaster management</td><td>Mapping flood-prone areas and planning emergency response routes</td></tr><tr><td>Agriculture</td><td>Precision farming, soil mapping and irrigation planning</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Importance of GIS</h3><ul style=\"margin-left:20px;\"><li>Allows complex spatial data to be visualised and understood easily through maps.</li><li>Supports better-informed decision-making in planning and policy.</li><li>Helps identify patterns and relationships not obvious from raw data alone.</li><li>Integrates data from multiple sources (satellite imagery, surveys, census data) into a single system.</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span> <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be able to list the five components of GIS — hardware, software, data, people and methods — as this is a frequently tested framework.</div>",
